Chapter 6 — Part 1
Curtis had just stepped clear of the scrap pile when Aizawa dropped from the observation walkway, capture weapon trailing, landing light on the cracked pavement a few meters ahead.
The class noise from above faded as the teacher straightened. Dust still drifted between them. Aizawa’s eyes ran a quick check—cuts on the arms, gym clothes torn at one shoulder, breathing already even—then settled on Curtis’s face.
“You done showing off?”
Curtis rolled the shoulder that had taken the worst of the charge through the second bot. “Wasn’t showing off. You wanted a measurement. You got one.”
“I wanted to know if you’d freeze or sandbag when the things actually tried to kill you.” Aizawa’s voice stayed flat. “You didn’t. You also put two very expensive machines through each other and turned a city block into rubble. Nezu’s already calculating the repair line.”
“Figured the budget could handle it if he signed off on three of them.” Curtis met his eyes. “They telegraphed every move. Once the first one was down the other two didn’t adjust. Not much else to it.”
Aizawa was quiet for a second. The distant chatter from the deck kept going—Mina’s voice, Bakugo’s sharper one, Midoriya still muttering. He jerked his chin toward the exit tunnel.
“Locker room. Clean up. Then my office. We’re rewriting your training limits before someone gets the bright idea to pair you with the rest of them at full output.” His eyes narrowed. “And next time I say don’t sandbag, I mean in the exercise I actually assign. Not whatever personal standard you’re using from the mountains.”
Curtis nodded once. “Understood.”
Aizawa turned and started walking. Curtis followed, boots crunching over concrete fragments, the low unnamed pull in his chest as steady as it had been all morning. Above them the class was still pressed to the glass, watching the two of them leave the ruined intersection behind.
Curtis kept pace beside him through the exit tunnel, voice low enough that it stayed between them.
“You said don’t sandbag. I only used what the situation needed. Maybe I got a little carried away at the end—I do like a good fight.” He glanced over. “But if you’re looking to test my actual limits, they won’t show up here. Not against machines that announce every punch. I’m sure Principal Nezu already explained that part.”
Aizawa didn’t stop walking. The capture weapon shifted against his shoulders. “He explained enough. Doesn’t mean I have to like training someone whose ceiling isn’t on school grounds. You’re still in my class. That means I still decide what ‘necessary’ looks like when other students are in the blast radius.”
They reached the locker room door. Aizawa paused with one hand on it, eyes tired and sharp.
“Clean up. Office in ten. And Beardy—if you ever decide a fight is ‘good’ enough to stop calculating collateral, you and I are going to have a much louder conversation. Nezu’s clearance doesn’t cover that.”
Curtis nodded, expression even. “Fair.”
Aizawa pushed the door open and left him to it. The tunnel hummed with distant ventilation. Curtis stepped inside, already peeling the torn gym shirt off one shoulder, the low steady pull in his chest unchanged while the rest of the class finally started filing down from the deck behind him.
Aizawa’s office was the same as last time—dim, coffee gone cold, sleeping bag in the corner. He didn’t sit. He stood behind the desk with a fresh tablet open to the Ground Beta feed, capture weapon draped over the chair.
Curtis stepped in, hair still damp from the quick rinse, uniform swapped clean. He shut the door and stayed standing.
“Limits,” Aizawa said. No warm-up. “You told me they won’t be tested here. Nezu said the same thing in fewer words. That leaves me planning around a variable I can’t fully measure.” He set the tablet down. “So here’s how this works. In class exercises you operate at the level that lets the other nineteen students actually train. Not the level that turns the arena into scrap. Outside of that—if something real shows up—you use whatever you actually have. I don’t want hero students dying because you decided today was another day to hold the mountain standard.”
Curtis’s expression stayed even. “I can do that.”
“You’d better. Because the second I think you’re sandbagging in a way that puts someone else at risk, or going overboard in a way that does the same, we’re back in here with Nezu on the call.” Aizawa’s eyes were steady. “You’re not a special case. You’re a student with a clearance note. Those expire the moment they become a problem.”
“Understood.”
Aizawa studied him another second, then jerked his chin at the door. “Go. Class is still on the deck pretending they’re not waiting to swarm you with questions. Deal with it or don’t. Just don’t start another crater on the way.”
Curtis turned, opened the door, and stepped back into the hallway. The low, steady pull in his chest stayed exactly where it always was—quiet, unnamed, and completely irrelevant to the rules Aizawa had just set.
The hallway outside Aizawa’s office was empty for the moment. Curtis walked a few steps, shoulders loose, face the same even mask he’d worn on the field. Then the quiet stretched. His hand tightened once on the duffel strap.
“I don’t know why God made me like this…”
The words were low, almost under his breath. He stopped. For a second the calm slipped—just enough that the confusion sat visible in the line of his mouth.
“Who are you talking to?”
Curtis turned. Uraraka stood a few paces back, cheeks already a little pink, one hand half-raised like she’d almost waved. Midoriya was beside her, notebook tucked under his arm, green eyes wide with the same open curiosity he always carried.
The mask slid back into place between one breath and the next. Curtis’s posture eased. The small, wry edge returned to his voice.
“Oh, nobody. Just reciting some song lyrics.” He shifted the duffel. “Sup?”
Uraraka blinked, then smiled, a bit flustered. “We were heading back from the deck. Everyone’s still talking about the fight. Iida’s trying to organize a debrief and Mina’s already planning a watch party for the footage.” Her eyes flicked over him, quick. “You okay? That was… a lot.”
Midoriya nodded, earnest. “Aizawa looked serious when he pulled you. If you need the notes from today’s schedule or anything, I wrote most of it down.”
Curtis huffed a short breath, almost a laugh. “I’m good. Just ready for the questions to be over. You two heading to class or ditching?”
“Class,” Uraraka said, still smiling. “Come on. We’ll run interference if Bakugo starts yelling again.”
She fell in on one side. Midoriya took the other. Curtis walked with them, calm restored, the quiet confusion from a moment ago already locked back down where no one else could see it. The low, steady pull in his chest stayed exactly where it always was.
The rest of the morning passed in ordinary blocks—Present Mic’s English, Midnight’s art history, Cementoss on modern literature. No one mentioned the zero-pointers. By the time the bell rang for Foundational Hero Studies the class was already buzzing again.
All Might met them at the Ground Beta gates in full costume, smile wide, cape snapping. “I AM HERE! And today, young heroes, you finally get to wear the real thing!”
They changed in the provided lockers. When the class reassembled on the training ground the costumes made a bright, uneven line—Bakugo’s grenade gauntlets, Uraraka’s pink and black, Todoroki’s half-and-half, Iida’s armored engines, Mina’s skin-tight acid suit, Momo’s high-collared red.
Curtis stepped out last.
The costume stopped conversation. Dark layered armor with a scaled, almost organic texture in black and deep green. Spiked pauldrons. A heavy, tattered cloak that moved like something grown instead of sewn. The mask was a bone-white deer skull with branching antlers, long braids spilling from beneath it. The eye sockets glowed—one red, one blue—matching the heterochromia underneath. He stood a full head taller than most of them even without the antlers.
Mina whistled. “Okay, Crypt King. You win the entrance.”
Uraraka’s cheeks went pink the second the mask’s glow landed on her. Momo’s gaze lingered on the armor’s construction, analytical and a little warmer than usual. Even Jiro’s earjacks twitched.
All Might’s smile didn’t falter, but his eyes sharpened with recognition. “Excellent costumes, all of you! Young Beardy, that is… certainly distinctive.”
“Does the job,” Curtis said, voice slightly filtered through the mask.
All Might clapped his hands once. “Listen up! Most villain encounters happen indoors. Today we train for that. You’ll be split into teams of two—heroes versus villains. The villains are guarding a nuclear weapon they plan to detonate. Heroes win by capturing both villains or securing the core before time runs out. Villains win by protecting the core the full time or capturing both heroes. Capture tape will be provided. You’ll draw lots for partners and matchups. Five minutes for the villain team to set up. Then the heroes move in. Go beyond! PLUS ULTRA!”
The class erupted into noise—excitement, nerves, Bakugo already cracking his knuckles. Curtis stood quiet at the edge of the group, antlers casting long shadows, the low steady pull in his chest unchanged while more than one classmate kept glancing his way.
Curtis tilted his head, antlers shifting. The mask’s eye slits glowed steady.
“Plus Ultra? I heard Star and Stripe say that.”
The class noise dipped for a second. Midoriya’s head snapped around, green eyes wide. He’d been half-reaching for his capture tape and froze instead, notebook already threatening to reappear.
“You—you know Star and Stripe?” Midoriya’s voice jumped. “And you’ve seen All Might’s videos, right? He’s the number one hero, the Symbol of Peace, his debut alone redefined—”
Curtis shrugged, armor plates moving with the motion. “Seen some of the clips. Cathleen talks about him. He’s strong.” The filtered voice stayed even. “But I’m actually a big fan of the Rabbit Hero: Mirko. Independent. No agency. Never went through U.A. Just does the work. That’s the kind of hero I pay attention to.”
Midoriya’s mouth opened, then closed. The surprise was written clear across his face—someone standing in All Might’s class, wearing a full hero costume, and naming a different pro as their favorite without hesitation. Uraraka glanced between them, cheeks still faintly pink. Bakugo scoffed from further down the line.
All Might’s smile held, but something quieter moved behind it. He’d already heard the reason once, in a clearing with a fresh crater. He didn’t repeat it here.
“Mirko is an exceptional hero,” he said, voice still bright. “Young Beardy has good taste. Now—lots! Let’s see who fights who!”
Midoriya was still staring. Curtis met his eyes through the mask for a moment, then looked toward the drawing boxes like the conversation was already finished. The low, steady pull in his chest stayed exactly where it always was.
All Might held up the two boxes, smile wide. “Teams and matchups are decided by lottery! No choosing your friends today—real hero work doesn’t work that way!”
Students drew. Paper slips unfolded. All Might read them off in his booming voice until he reached the one that made the deck go quiet.
“First match! Team A—Curtis Beardy and Ochaco Uraraka—as the heroes! Versus Team D—Katsuki Bakugo and Tenya Iida—as the villains!”
Bakugo’s palms popped instantly. “Hell yes. Finally get to wipe that look off the extra’s face.”
Iida adjusted his glasses, engines already idling. “We will prepare a perfect defense. The weapon will not be taken.”
Uraraka’s cheeks flushed the moment her name landed next to Curtis’s. She glanced up at the antlered mask, then quickly at the building. “W-we’ll do our best!”
Curtis just nodded once. “Alright.”
All Might gestured toward the replica building. “Villain team, you have five minutes to locate the nuclear payload and set your defenses! Hero team, you’ll receive the building schematic out here. Use the time wisely! Go beyond—PLUS ULTRA!”
Bakugo and Iida took off at once, Iida’s engines flaring as they disappeared through the entrance. Inside, they cleared floors fast. Iida’s Recipro Burst let him sweep entire hallways in seconds while Bakugo kicked doors and snarled at every empty room. They found the fake missile on the fifth floor, center room, propped on a stand like a stage prop. Iida immediately began calculating choke points and capture-tape angles. Bakugo planted himself in the main corridor, gauntlets primed, grinning sharp.
“Let ’em come. I’m blowing that masked extra through the wall the second he shows his face.”
Outside, Uraraka accepted the schematic from All Might with both hands, still pink. Curtis stood beside her, mask tilted as he studied the paper over her shoulder, already tracing entry routes while the five-minute clock started.
The five minutes ended. All Might’s voice crackled over the earpieces. “Hero team, you may enter! Time starts now!”
Curtis and Uraraka moved through the ground-floor entrance together. She floated a few loose pieces of debris out of their path, light on her feet. He stayed half a step ahead, antlers nearly brushing the hallway ceiling, mask’s eyes sweeping corners.
They didn’t make it far.
A blast tore through the stairwell door two floors up. Bakugo came through the smoke already airborne, gauntlets whining, grin feral.
“FOUND YOU!”
He ignored the plan, ignored Iida’s sharp “Bakugo, return to position!” over the shared channel, and shot straight down the corridor at Curtis. Explosions cracked off the walls as he closed the distance, capture tape forgotten, every ounce of focus locked on the masked exchange student.
Iida’s engines echoed somewhere above, frustrated and already recalculating. “Bakugo! The objective is the weapon! Do not abandon the defense!”
Bakugo didn’t answer. He just laughed, sharp and loud, and hurled himself at Curtis with both palms lit.
“I’m taking you down first, extra! Then the girl! Then the damn bomb!”
Curtis set his feet, cloak shifting, and raised one armored forearm as the first explosion rolled in. Uraraka yelped and ducked behind a support pillar, hands already glowing, ready to lighten anything that came her way.
The hallway shook. Bakugo had left his partner behind without a second thought. The only thing he wanted was the fight.
The explosion never landed.
Curtis took one step forward. His fist shot out, short and exact, and cracked Bakugo square in the philtrum. The impact snapped Bakugo’s head back. His eyes rolled. The gauntlets powered down mid-blast as his body dropped, unconscious before he hit the floor.
Silence.
In the monitoring room All Might and Aizawa both hit the replay at the same time. The footage crawled. One frame Bakugo was inbound, palms lit. The next Curtis had already closed the gap, fist extended, the strike landing clean under the nose. No wind-up. No wasted motion. Bakugo’s momentum did the rest.
Aizawa exhaled through his nose. “Philtrum. Lights out. He didn’t even brace.”
All Might’s smile had gone thin. “Young Bakugo never saw it.”
On the feed Curtis crouched, pulled the capture tape from his belt, and bound Bakugo’s wrists and ankles with quick, practiced loops. He stood, rolled the shoulder that had thrown the punch, and turned toward the pillar.
“Let us proceed, Ochaco.”
Uraraka peeked out, eyes wide, cheeks flushed. She nodded fast and floated after him as he started toward the stairs, leaving Bakugo tied and out cold in the hallway behind them. Above, Iida’s engines were already changing direction. The weapon was still five floors up. The hero team had just removed half the defense in a single step.
They paused on the fourth-floor landing. Curtis kept his voice low, mask tilted toward Uraraka.
“In this room Iida’s guarding the bomb. He’s fast. If his engines get going he could match my base speed.” He nodded at the debris scattered along the hallway. “I’m going to smash rubble around the room. Use your anti-gravity on it. I’ll rush Iida and keep him busy. You jump off the pillars and get to the bomb.”
Uraraka’s eyes widened, then sharpened. She pressed her fingertips together, already nodding. “Got it. I’ll float the pieces the second you break them. Just… don’t let him line up a Recipro Burst on you.”
“Wasn’t planning on it.”
Curtis rolled his neck once. The antlers nearly brushed the ceiling. He set a hand on the stairwell door, listening to the faint idle of engines on the other side.
“Ready?”
“Ready.”
He pushed through. Uraraka followed, hands glowing, already picking her first pillar.
Curtis hit the fifth-floor door shoulder-first. The first two support pillars cracked under armored fists; chunks of concrete tore free and hung weightless the second Uraraka’s fingers brushed them. He didn’t slow. Engines whined as Iida planted himself between Curtis and the missile, armor gleaming, capture tape ready.
“I will not let you pass!”
Curtis closed the distance in three strides, fist already drawn back. Iida braced, Recipro engines flaring—
Curtis stopped. He lowered his hand and simply pointed behind Iida.
Uraraka stood at the missile stand, one palm flat on the core, the other raised in a small, breathless wave. Floating rubble drifted around her like lazy satellites. The objective light on the wall had already flipped green.
All Might’s voice boomed over every earpiece. “THE HERO TEAM HAS SECURED THE NUCLEAR PAYLOAD! TEAM A WINS!”
Iida’s engines cut. He stared at the bomb, then at Uraraka, then back at Curtis. His posture straightened into a formal bow. “The objective was achieved. Well executed.”
Curtis nodded once. “Good fight.”
On the monitoring screens the class erupted—Mina cheering, Midoriya scribbling so fast his pencil tore paper, Bakugo still unconscious and tied in the hallway below while Recovery Girl’s drone already headed his way. Uraraka hopped down from the stand, cheeks bright pink, and flashed Curtis a grin as the rubble gently settled around them.
The class filled the monitoring room while Recovery Girl’s drone carted Bakugo off for a precautionary check. All Might stood at the main screen, smile still present but measured. Aizawa leaned against the back wall, capture weapon loose, tablet in hand.
“First match review,” All Might said. “Team A versus Team D. Hero team: Beardy and Uraraka. Villain team: Bakugo and Iida.”
He pulled up the key frames. “Young Iida maintained the objective and adapted when his partner left position. Excellent spatial awareness. Young Bakugo…” All Might’s smile tightened. “Abandoned the plan for a direct confrontation and was neutralized instantly. Reckless. We’ll discuss impulse control when he wakes up.”
Aizawa didn’t look up from the tablet. “Uraraka executed the flanking route cleanly once the debris was available. Good timing. Minimal hesitation. Beardy disabled the primary aggressor in one strike and created the opening without excess property damage after the initial pillars. Efficient.” His eyes flicked to Curtis. “You still turned a stairwell into rubble. Keep the collateral inside the exercise parameters next time.”
All Might nodded. “Overall: Hero team victory by objective. Strong coordination from Uraraka and Beardy. Villain team failed to maintain formation. Iida, you did what you could. Bakugo, we’ll talk.” He clapped once, energy returning. “Next teams, get ready! The rest of you—take notes! This is how you improve!”
The class buzzed again. Uraraka ducked her head, smiling. Curtis stood at the back with his arms crossed, mask off now, expression even while Aizawa’s grade sat on the record and the next lottery already spun.
Bakugo slammed back into the monitoring room still half-wrapped in capture tape he’d ripped through, Recovery Girl’s protests echoing behind him. His palms crackled the second he saw Curtis at the back wall.
“YOU—!”
He crossed the room in a burst of explosions, students scattering. Both hands locked onto Curtis’s collar and yanked him forward, gauntlets scraping armor. “You got a lucky shot, you masked extra! I wasn’t ready! Next time I’m blowing your damn antlers off—don’t think tying me up means you won!”
The room went sharp. All Might’s smile vanished. Aizawa’s capture weapon twitched.
Curtis didn’t raise his hands. He looked down at Bakugo, expression even, voice low through the lingering mask filter. “You left your partner. I hit what was in front of me. That’s the match.”
Bakugo’s grip tightened, explosions popping against the costume. “Shut up! I’m not done—!”
Aizawa’s scarf snapped out and wrapped Bakugo’s wrists before the next blast could build. “Enough. You lost because you abandoned the objective. Touch him again and you’re running laps until graduation.”
All Might stepped in, one large hand settling on Bakugo’s shoulder. “Young Bakugo. Release him. We will discuss this properly after class. Not here.”
Bakugo snarled but let go, chest heaving, eyes still locked on Curtis. Curtis straightened his collar, rolled one shoulder, and stayed exactly where he was. The low, steady pull in his chest hadn’t moved. Neither had he.
Midoriya stepped forward before All Might could fully steer Bakugo toward the door. His hands were half-raised, notebook forgotten, voice careful the way it always got when he spoke to the boy he’d known since they were small.
“Kacchan… you don’t have to—”
Bakugo’s head snapped around. “Don’t. Don’t you start with that soft crap, Deku.”
Midoriya didn’t back off. The old habit sat right there in his chest—the same one that had made him reach out when they were kids, even after the river, even after every explosion that followed. “I’m not looking down on you. You left the plan and he countered it. That’s all. You’re still— you can still—”
“Still what?” Bakugo’s palms popped, scarf creaking where Aizawa held him. “Still better than you? I already know that. I don’t need the useless nerd who used to chase me around telling me how to lose.” His voice cracked sharper. “You always did this. Acted like you were helping when you were just standing there feeling sorry for me. I hated it then. I hate it now.”
Midoriya’s mouth opened, then closed. The words he wanted—about the river, about never thinking Bakugo was actually better, about still wanting him to win the right way—stuck behind his teeth. He lowered his hands.
“I’m not feeling sorry for you, Kacchan. I just… don’t want you to get hurt because you stopped thinking.”
Bakugo scoffed, raw and angry, and jerked against the capture weapon. “Save it. Go write it in your damn notebook with the rest of your hero fantasies.”
All Might’s hand tightened gently on his shoulder. Aizawa’s eyes stayed flat. Midoriya stayed where he was, green eyes steady and a little sad, while Bakugo kept glaring past him at Curtis like the only loss that mattered was the one he could still blow up.
Curtis clapped once. The sound cut clean through the room.
“Enough. Mr. Aizawa. Mr. All Might.” His voice stayed level, mask off, eyes on the two teachers. “I believe you were about to announce the MVP of the lesson.”
The tension stalled. Bakugo’s snarl caught halfway. Midoriya blinked. Aizawa’s capture weapon stayed wrapped, but his gaze shifted to Curtis with something close to approval for the redirect.
All Might recovered first, smile returning as he straightened. “Right you are, young Beardy! For this match, the MVP is—” He glanced at the screen, then at the class. “Ochaco Uraraka! She followed the plan under pressure, used her quirk exactly when it counted, and secured the objective while her partner created the opening. Excellent work!”
Uraraka went bright red, hands flying to her cheeks. “E-eh?! Me?”
Aizawa grunted. “She didn’t chase the fight. She finished the job. That’s the standard.” His eyes flicked to Bakugo. “Something the rest of you can learn from. Especially you.”
Bakugo looked ready to explode again, but the scarf tightened just enough to keep his hands down. Curtis stepped back, arms loose, expression even while the class buzzed around Uraraka’s flustered thanks and All Might moved on to the next teams. The low, steady pull in his chest stayed quiet. The moment had passed.
Curtis looked over at Uraraka and smiled—small, genuine, the kind that reached his eyes.
“Well deserved. Congratulations.”
Uraraka’s face went from pink to scarlet. She ducked her head, fingers twisting together, the Free Love warmth making the simple words land heavier than they should have. “T-thank you! I just— I wouldn’t have reached it without the opening you made, so…”
She trailed off, smiling too wide to finish. Mina immediately slung an arm around her shoulders with a grin. Midoriya was already scribbling the MVP note. Curtis gave her one short nod and turned his attention back to All Might as the next matchup was called, the low steady pull in his chest as quiet as ever.
